---
import ArticleClosingNote from '@/components/articles/ArticleClosingNote.astro';

const prose = 'article-prose max-w-3xl text-black/80 [&_p]:mb-5 [&_p:last-child]:mb-0';
---

<section id="section-0" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Kamakura Day Trip Overview</h3>
  </div>
  <div class="max-w-3xl rounded-lg bg-[#F5F2EB] px-6 py-5 text-md leading-relaxed text-black/80">
    <p class="mb-3"><span class="font-medium text-black">📍 Destination:</span> Kamakura, Kanagawa</p>
    <p class="mb-3"><span class="font-medium text-black">🚆 Best way to get there:</span> JR Yokosuka Line from Tokyo Station, or other train routes depending on where you are staying</p>
    <p class="mb-3"><span class="font-medium text-black">⏱️ Suggested time:</span> Half-day or full-day trip</p>
    <p class="mb-3"><span class="font-medium text-black">🚃 Main transport in Kamakura:</span> Walking + Enoden Line, depending on your route</p>
    <p class="mb-3"><span class="font-medium text-black">⭐ Main highlights:</span> Hasedera, Kotoku-in, Komachi Street</p>
    <p><span class="font-medium text-black">➕ Optional stops:</span> Goryo Shrine, Hokokuji Temple, Shichirigahama Beach</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-1" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>How to Get to Kamakura from Tokyo</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Kamakura is easy to reach from Tokyo by train, which makes it one of the most convenient day trips from the city. The exact route depends on where you are staying, but most visitors arrive at Kamakura Station first.</p>
    <p>From Kamakura Station, you can take the Enoden Line toward your first stop. This is what I did for my own Kamakura trip, and Google Maps made it easy to check train times, platforms, and connections along the way.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-2" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Breakfast at Yoridokoro</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Yoridokoro is a lovely place to start your Kamakura day trip, especially if you want something more local than a quick convenience store breakfast. It is known for its Japanese-style breakfast, usually served with grilled fish, rice, miso soup, pickles, and a fresh egg, but what makes it especially popular is its location right beside the Enoden train line.</p>
    <p>If you get one of the counter seats by the window, you can watch the Enoden train pass right in front of you while you eat. These seats are limited, so I recommend making a reservation if the train view is important to you. We visited around opening time and did not have to wait long, but Yoridokoro is a popular stop, so I would still arrive early.</p>
    <p>To be honest, I never expected to enjoy fish for breakfast as much as I did here, and that is exactly why I would absolutely recommend it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-3" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Goryo Shrine</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Goryo Shrine is not a place you visit for taking photos, as photography is not allowed inside the shrine grounds. This is one of the reasons it felt so quiet, local, and much less crowded than some of the more famous temples nearby, and it ended up being one of the most memorable experiences of my Kamakura trip.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-4" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Hasedera Temple</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Hasedera is one of Kamakura's most beautiful temple stops, and it is definitely worth adding to your day. The temple grounds are lovely to explore, with gardens, small statues, wooden buildings, and viewpoints over Kamakura and the sea. Be prepared to spend more time here than you might expect, especially if the weather is nice. There is enough to see that you will probably want to wander for a while before moving on.</p>
    <p>There is a small entrance fee to visit Hasedera, currently ¥400 for adults, but you do not need to book tickets in advance.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-5" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Kotoku-in & the Great Buddha of Kamakura</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Kotoku-in is home to the famous Great Buddha of Kamakura, one of the most iconic sights in the area. The temple grounds felt quite small to me, so this does not need to be a long stop, but the Great Buddha is still absolutely worth seeing in person, especially if it is your first time visiting Kamakura. There is a small entrance fee to visit Kotoku-in, currently ¥300 for adults.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-6" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Komachi Street</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Komachi Street, near Kamakura Station, is the best place to go later in the day for street food, cafés, sweets, souvenirs, and small local shops. It feels much livelier than the temple areas, so it is a good place to browse, grab a snack, and enjoy a different side of Kamakura before heading back to Tokyo.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-7" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Optional: Hokokuji Temple</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>If you still have time and energy, Hokokuji Temple is a beautiful optional stop, especially if you want to see its bamboo garden. I did not visit it on my own Kamakura trip, so I would treat it as an extra rather than an essential stop for this route, but it is a lovely choice if you want to add one more temple experience and have matcha with bamboo views before heading back.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-8" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Optional Sunset Stop: Shichirigahama Beach</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>If the weather is good, Shichirigahama Beach can be a beautiful way to end your Kamakura day trip by the sea, especially if you enjoy coastal views, golden-hour light, and a quieter finish before heading back to Tokyo.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-9" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class={prose}>
    <ArticleClosingNote label="Before you go">
      <p>If you are planning your own Kamakura day trip, I recommend choosing your main stops first, then adding any optional places depending on your time, energy, and the weather. That way, the day feels manageable instead of rushed.</p>
      <p>I've pinned the places mentioned in this guide, along with extra cafés, food spots, attractions, and day trip ideas, on my Ultimate Tokyo Map. You can find it inside my 5-day Tokyo itinerary, save it for your trip, and use it to see where everything is located so you can plan your route more easily.</p>
    </ArticleClosingNote>
  </div>
</section>
